course: world
lesson: 7
title: The shape and size of the Earth, the poles and the equator
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The Earth is a ball, very slightly flattened at the top and bottom; it looks flat to us only because it is so big and we see a tiny part of it.
explain: The two ends of the Earth's axis are the North Pole and the South Pole, and the equator is an imaginary line around the middle, halfway between them.
explain: The equator splits the Earth into the Northern and Southern Hemispheres; the equator is about 40 thousand kilometres long, so a car driving day and night at 100 kilometres an hour would need more than 16 days to go around.
explain: People knew the Earth was round more than two thousand years ago: ships sink hull first (the hull is the bottom part of a ship) below the horizon, the far line where the sky meets the sea, and the Earth's shadow on the Moon during an eclipse is always round.
explain: Today photographs from space show the round Earth, and people have sailed and flown all the way around it.
fact: The Earth is shaped like a ball, very slightly flattened at the poles.
fact: The distance around the Earth at the equator is about 40 thousand kilometres.
fact: The Earth is about 12 thousand 700 kilometres wide.
fact: The North Pole and the South Pole are the two ends of the Earth's axis.
fact: The equator is an imaginary line around the Earth, halfway between the poles.
fact: The equator divides the Earth into the Northern and Southern Hemispheres.
fact: Places near the equator are hot, and places near the poles are cold.
fact: About seven tenths of the Earth's surface is covered by water.
fact: The North Pole is in the middle of a frozen ocean, and the South Pole is on the icy land of Antarctica.
fact: A ship sailing away disappears from the bottom up because the Earth's surface curves.
fact: The Earth's shadow on the Moon is always round, which shows the Earth is a ball.
fact: A globe is a small model of the Earth.
exercise: What shape is the Earth? | A ball, slightly flattened at the poles | A flat disc with edges | A long egg shape with its point towards the Sun | A cube with rounded corners
exercise: Why does the Earth look flat when we stand on it? | It is so big that we see only a tiny part | It really is flat where people live | Its curve is only at the poles | The air makes it look flat
exercise: What is the equator? | An imaginary line around the middle of the Earth | A real wall that people built all around the Earth | The line from one pole to the other | The edge between day and night
exercise: Where are the poles? | At the two ends of the Earth's axis | On the equator | In the middle of the oceans | Wherever it is coldest on the Earth each year
exercise: How long is the equator? | About 40 thousand kilometres | About 400 kilometres | About 4 thousand kilometres | About 4 million kilometres
exercise: What does the equator divide the Earth into? | The Northern and Southern Hemispheres | The Eastern and Western Hemispheres | The land and the sea | The day side and the night side
exercise: Why is it colder near the poles? | Sunlight there comes in at a low slant | The poles are much farther away from the Sun | The poles get no sunlight ever | Cold winds come from space there
exercise: What lies at the South Pole? | The icy land of Antarctica | A frozen ocean with no land | A hot desert | A tall mountain of rock
exercise: What lies at the North Pole? | Ice floating on an ocean | A big frozen continent of rock and ice | A small town of scientists and their huts | A forest of pine trees and snowy hills
exercise: How much of the Earth's surface is covered by water? | About seven tenths | About one half of it | Only about one tenth | Almost all of it, everywhere
exercise: Why does a ship going away seem to sink from the bottom up? | The Earth's surface is curved | The ship really sinks a little | The waves hide the bottom first | The air bends only the bottom
exercise: What did ancient people notice about the Earth's shadow on the Moon? | It is always round | It is always square | It changes shape each time | It is always a thin line
exercise: What is a globe? | A small round model of the Earth | A flat map of one country on paper | A round model of the Sun and its light | A picture of the stars in the night sky
exercise: How wide is the Earth through its middle? | About 12 thousand 700 kilometres | About 1 thousand 270 kilometres | About 127 thousand kilometres | About 127 kilometres
test: A traveller flies east and keeps going without turning. What happens? | She comes back to where she started | She falls off the edge of the Earth | She reaches the North Pole | She flies away into space
test: Which is true? | The equator is halfway between the North and South Poles | The equator passes through the North Pole | The equator runs from pole to pole | The equator runs all the way from the North Pole to the South Pole
test: Max stands on the beach and watches a sailing ship go away. What does he see last? | The top of the mast | The bottom of the ship | The middle of the ship | All of it vanishes at once
test: A country lies on the equator. What weather is most likely there? | Warm all year round | Snow most of the year | Very cold winters | Ice all year round
test: Why do photographs from space show a round Earth? | Because the Earth is a ball | Because cameras make everything round | Because space is round | Because the Moon is in the way
test: A penguin lives near the South Pole. In which hemisphere does it live? | The Southern Hemisphere | The Northern Hemisphere | On the equator | In both at once
test: If you could walk around the equator at about 30 kilometres a day, how long would it take? | More than three years | About one month of walking | About one week if you hurry | About thirty long years
test: Which shows best that the Earth is round? | Its shadow on the Moon is always round | The sky is blue | The Sun is hot at noon | Rivers always flow downhill towards the sea
test: Where would you come out if you drilled from the North Pole straight through the Earth's centre? | You would come out at the South Pole | You would come out at the equator | You would come out in Africa | You would come out in Australia
test: how far round the world is it roughly | About 40 thousand kilometres | About 4 thousand kilometres | About 400 thousand kilometres | About 12 thousand kilometres
test: wots the equator again | An imaginary line round the middle, halfway between the poles | A real painted line you can stand on | The line you'd draw from the North Pole down to the South Pole | The edge of the world where the sea ends
test: a video said the Earth is flat and ships just get too small to see. true? | No, ships vanish from the bottom up because the Earth curves | Yes, ships just shrink until you can't see them | Yes, the sea is flat so ships only get smaller | No, ships sink down a little as they sail away
test: Leo says the South Pole is in the middle of a frozen ocean. right? | No, that's the North Pole; the South Pole is on Antarctica's land | Yes, both poles are frozen oceans | Yes, and it's the North Pole that sits on land, a big frozen continent | No, the South Pole is on a warm island
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the figure of the Earth? | A sphere slightly flattened at the poles | A perfectly flat disc | A sphere elongated at the poles | A cube with rounded edges
exam: State the approximate circumference of the Earth measured at the equator. | Forty thousand kilometres | Four thousand kilometres | Four hundred thousand kilometres | Twelve thousand seven hundred kilometres
exam: State the approximate proportion of the Earth's surface covered by water. | Seven tenths | One half | Three tenths | Nine tenths
exam: Identify which of the following observations constitutes evidence that the surface of the Earth is curved. | A departing vessel vanishes from the hull upward | A departing vessel diminishes uniformly until invisible | A departing vessel vanishes from the mast downward | A departing vessel remains visible indefinitely
